{
  "abstract": "For $n\\le74$ and $n=76$ the no-three-in-line optimum is known: $t(n)=2n$. Above that range the best values come from local search, and they fall from about $1.84n$ at $n=100$ to $1.65n$ at $n=1600$. Whether this decay says anything about $\\lim t(n)/n$ has been left open. We run three local-search methods behind that curve where the answer is known: structure-free iterated greedy, quarter-turn plateau search under two budget laws, and an exact quarter-turn large-neighbourhood search. All three miss $2n$ from $n=40$ on, by 6–15% at $n=60$–$76$, with no downward trend and seed spreads smaller than the gaps. At its published budget the strongest method gives $140=1.84n$ at $n=76$, the same ratio it gives at $n=100$. Quarter-turn $2n$ solutions exist at every size tested, and their number grows with $n$ where enumerated. A structured control behaves differently: in a box lift class, iterated greedy matches the exact optimum at every proved instance, up to 676 candidate points. With 30 times the budget the plateau method gains one orbit at $n=60$ and at $n=76$ and still misses $2n$. So at $n\\le76$ the decay of local-search values reflects the methods at these budgets, not $t(n)$; the value at $n=100$ is consistent with a true ratio of $2$; and search-found values on large structured supports are lower bounds of unknown slack. Nothing here bounds $t(n)$ or concerns $n\\gg100$.",
